00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.160
hello and welcome i'm james murphy. did you&nbsp;
know that not all exceptions in python derive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.680
from the built-in Exception class? that's&nbsp;
right not all exceptions are Exceptions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.640
this is almost correct though, let's see what&nbsp;
happens if we try to raise something that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:23.840
not an Exception. here i try raising a string and&nbsp;
i get an error message that says all exceptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:31.200
must derive from BaseException, not Exception.&nbsp;
okay so all exceptions have to be BaseExceptions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.880
but what's the difference between a BaseException&nbsp;
and an Exception? well here's the official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:42.160
documentation. a BaseException is just the&nbsp;
base class for all built-in exceptions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.960
on the other hand, a plain old Exception is for&nbsp;
all built-in non-system exiting exceptions, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.600
all user-defined exceptions. clearly&nbsp;
the python documentation is telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.480
me that for my own exceptions i should be&nbsp;
inheriting from Exception not BaseException.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.600
so what are the built-in system exiting&nbsp;
exceptions that it's talking about here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.320
we can find out a little bit lower in the&nbsp;
documentation by looking at the exception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.240
class hierarchy. from this list we can see that&nbsp;
python has only three built-in exceptions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.880
are BaseExceptions but not Exceptions.&nbsp;
looking at the first two, now we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.840
what the authors of the documentation meant&nbsp;
when they said non-system exiting exceptions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.920
SystemExit and KeyboardInterrupt generally cause&nbsp;
your python program to stop. GeneratorExit on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.400
other hand doesn't cause your program to stop&nbsp;
and i'm not quite sure why it's a BaseException&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:40.000
and not just an Exception. my intuition tells&nbsp;
me that it should go wherever StopIteration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.800
goes, but i'm sure there were good reasons for&nbsp;
doing this. but let's move on and stick with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.080
the main intent of separating BaseException from&nbsp;
Exception. let's investigate why it's useful to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:56.800
separate exiting exceptions from non-exiting ones.&nbsp;
consider the following example. suppose somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:02.560
deep down in our code something fails and we&nbsp;
want to exit the program, so we call sys.exit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.240
here we've wrapped the whole thing in a try&nbsp;
except where we're catching all Exceptions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.680
as you can see none of the print statements&nbsp;
execute. that's because sys.exit raised a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:23.200
SystemExit exception which is a BaseException, not&nbsp;
an Exception. what we're assuming here and what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:29.040
normally the case when you're programming is that&nbsp;
for exceptions you want to catch logical errors or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.480
failures of functions to succeed. handling these&nbsp;
kind of business logic exceptions is generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:40.000
what you mean to do when you use a try except.&nbsp;
this is a very different type of situation than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.920
what's going on if somewhere in the code someone&nbsp;
tries to exit the program. generally speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:51.440
if someone's trying to exit the program you&nbsp;
really shouldn't be stopping them. now of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:57.200
you can catch a SystemExit if you want to do&nbsp;
something specific in that situation, but normally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.240
when you say catch all the exceptions. this is not&nbsp;
what you have in mind. if you want to catch *all*&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:09.040
the exceptions, then you just use a bare except or&nbsp;
an except BaseException. keep in mind though that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:15.360
in my opinion catching all exceptions is almost&nbsp;
always a bad idea. in my opinion you should always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.600
prefer to use the most specific kind of exception&nbsp;
that you can think of. if you expect a TypeError&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:28.160
or a ValueError then use them. don't depend on&nbsp;
except BaseException or except Exception to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:33.040
catch everything. i know it's really tempting to&nbsp;
just use a bare except because it's less to type.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:40.000
using a bare except or an except Exception is way&nbsp;
too broad and it will often lead to catching and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:46.400
possibly silently handling errors that you weren't&nbsp;
expecting. like here i passed the wrong kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.680
argument to sys.exit, but during the execution&nbsp;
of the program i have no way of knowing that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.120
moving on, let's see what can go wrong if you&nbsp;
do try to catch all exceptions instead of just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.720
non-system-exiting ones. so i'm going ahead&nbsp;
and running this program, it's doing work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:07.760
it's doing work, it's doing work, and i decide&nbsp;
that it's taking too long. i want to kill this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:16.240
program so i go ahead and hit ctrl c and try to&nbsp;
kill it. no matter how many times i hit ctrl c&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.200
the exception handler is not allowing me to exit&nbsp;
the program. if i want to kill the program, i have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.640
to kill the terminal itself, or kill the python&nbsp;
process at the operating system level. however&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:33.520
if i just catch Exceptions, then i can kill it&nbsp;
with ctrl c, and i get a KeyboardInterrupt. this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.880
is a common mistake that people make especially&nbsp;
when accepting user input from the terminal. as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.920
a side note, if you really just want to kill&nbsp;
python and not ask it to shut down nicely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:50.880
you can do that using the os._exit function.&nbsp;
you can see that when we run this code nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.840
is printed out because python is killed. it&nbsp;
doesn't raise an exception like sys.exit does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:02.480
os._exit just kills the program. most of the&nbsp;
time, you only really want to do this after you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.360
forked a new process and then want to kill it,&nbsp;
but it's good to know just in case you have to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:14.720
finally, what's the deal with GeneratorExit?&nbsp;
GeneratorExit is not a system-exiting exception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:21.760
but it is a BaseException, not a Exception. a&nbsp;
GeneratorExit is raised inside a generator when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.200
you call the generator's close function before the&nbsp;
generator is finished. here we create a generator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:32.720
and call its next method once. it just yielded&nbsp;
0 and now it's paused. while it's paused we call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:37.760
its close method and we see that gen exit is&nbsp;
printed out. we see gen exit printed because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:43.120
the GeneratorExit was caught by this exception&nbsp;
handler. notice however that we re-raised the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:48.640
exception but we still don't see a trace back.&nbsp;
that's because much like a StopIteration used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:54.080
in for loops, a GeneratorExit is not really&nbsp;
an error. a generator raising GeneratorExit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:59.440
is just python's way of telling you that the&nbsp;
generator is done. python silently handles a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.800
GeneratorExit just like it silently handles a&nbsp;
StopIteration for a for loop. again, honestly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.440
i'm not sure why this is a BaseException and not&nbsp;
an Exception. in any case though that's all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.120
the built-in BaseExceptions in python that aren't&nbsp;
themselves Exceptions. you can technically make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:19.840
your own BaseExceptions by inheriting directly&nbsp;
from BaseException but the documentation tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.000
you that you shouldn't do this. well that's all&nbsp;
